#    180:    509: !       { ************************************************************************
#    180:    510: !
#    180:    511: !       Program object sizes and characteristics, sync with pint. These define
#    180:    512: !       the machine specific characteristics of the target.
#    180:    513: !
#    180:    514: !       The configurations are as follows:
#    180:    515: !
#    180:    516: !       type                  #bits 16    #bits 32  #bits 64
#    180:    517: !       ===========================================================
#    180:    518: !       integer               16          32        64
#    180:    519: !       real                  32          64        64
#    180:    520: !       char                  8           8         8
#    180:    521: !       boolean               8           8         8
#    180:    522: !       set                   256         256       256
#    180:    523: !       pointers              16          32        64
#    180:    524: !       marks                 16          32        64 (bytes)
#    180:    525: !       File logical number   8           8         8
#    180:    526: !
#    180:    527: !       Both endian types are supported. There is no alignment needed, but you
#    180:    528: !       may wish to use alignment to tune the runtime speed.
#    180:    529: !
#    180:    530: !       The machine characteristics dependent on byte accessable machines. This
#    180:    531: !       table is all you should need to adapt to any byte addressable machine.
#    180:    532: !
